<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<list xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="../../../../data/xsd/items.xsd">
	<!--
		Beast Collar taming items.

		displayId is the client-facing item identity, and this is an untouched
		Interlude client, so each one borrows a real client item record that has a
		suitable shape and icon. The logical names below are what the passport
		and the shop show, so a player never sees the borrowed name.

		9302 is the only item the server hands out by itself: it is created on a
		successful capture and is that pet's control item, exactly like a normal
		pet's collar. It cannot be traded, dropped, sold or given away, because
		the pet is bound to it.

		There are deliberately no technique tokens here. They were tried and do
		not work, for two independent reasons worth recording so nobody tries a
		third time.

		1. A pet's own inventory packet, PetItemList, has no displayId field. It
		   writes the raw item id, so the client resolves that id against its own
		   table; an id of ours draws a black icon with no use action. (The player
		   inventory packet does honour displayId, which is why the items above can
		   borrow one.) So a token has to BE an id the client already knows.

		2. A module item file cannot override a stock item id in this build, so
		   borrowing one is not enough either. ItemData parses base files and
		   module files into one ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet(), and ItemTemplate
		   defines neither equals nor hashCode - so it keys on identity. Both the
		   stock template and ours survive as separate entries and the final
		   _etcItems.put(id, template) pass lets the stock one win. A module
		   definition of 4411 is silently ignored. Reproduced against ItemData's
		   own merge order; this is not a load-order guess.

		Tame skills therefore live on the pet skill bar instead, which needs no
		item at all - see TameSkillBar for how the core reads them off the pet's
		own NPC template.
	-->

	<!-- Reagent for ordinary monsters. Using it on a wild creature fires Beast Binding. -->
	<item id="9300" type="EtcItem" name="Broken Beast Flute">
		<set name="icon" val="icon.etc_flute_i00"/>
		<set name="displayId" val="1770"/>
		<set name="default_action" val="SKILL_REDUCE"/>
		<set name="etcitem_type" val="POTION"/>
		<set name="immediate_effect" val="true"/>
		<set name="material" val="STEEL"/>
		<set name="weight" val="20"/>
		<set name="price" val="5000"/>
		<set name="is_stackable" val="true"/>
		<set name="is_tradable" val="true"/>
		<set name="is_dropable" val="true"/>
		<set name="is_sellable" val="true"/>
		<set name="is_depositable" val="true"/>
		<set name="handler" val="ItemSkills"/>
		<skills>
			<skill id="9300" level="1"/>
		</skills>
	</item>

	<!-- Reagent for raid monsters. -->
	<item id="9301" type="EtcItem" name="Ancient Beast Bugle">
		<set name="icon" val="icon.item_normal24"/>
		<set name="displayId" val="697"/>
		<set name="default_action" val="SKILL_REDUCE"/>
		<set name="etcitem_type" val="POTION"/>
		<set name="immediate_effect" val="true"/>
		<set name="material" val="STEEL"/>
		<set name="weight" val="20"/>
		<set name="price" val="50000"/>
		<set name="is_stackable" val="true"/>
		<set name="is_tradable" val="true"/>
		<set name="is_dropable" val="true"/>
		<set name="is_sellable" val="true"/>
		<set name="is_depositable" val="true"/>
		<set name="handler" val="ItemSkills"/>
		<skills>
			<skill id="9300" level="1"/>
		</skills>
	</item>

	<!--
		The control item, and the collar a worn beast answers to.

		9302 is the item id the server creates on a successful capture and the one
		stock pet code requires: PetDataTable looks a pet up BY ITEM ID, so the
		control item has to be an id the core's own pet table knows, which a module
		item id is not. So this borrows 118's item record - the one this module has
		always used for the control item, and the one the client is known to draw.
		That constraint is why the module's collar is not really its own item.

		'worn' is the one value in this file the core has no meaning for. Stock
		PET_COLLAR items have no worn state at all - a pet is either summoned or it
		is not, and there is no slot, appearance or equipment effect attached to
		wearing one. So the core is never told about it: the flag lives in this
		module's own row on the profile, and it decides which collar a recall brings
		back and which beasts are restored on login.

		It is read here only so the server knows the flag exists on the item. Setting
		it would be meaningless - ItemTemplate has no 'worn' set - and a set name the
		template does not recognise is dropped at load with a warning, so it is left
		out on purpose. See TameWearState for the state itself.
	-->
	<item id="9302" type="EtcItem" name="Tamed Beast Collar">
		<set name="icon" val="icon.etc_old_key_i02"/>
		<!--
			DO NOT REMOVE THIS. It is what makes the collar exist as far as the client
			is concerned.

			It was removed once, on the reasoning that no stock PET_COLLAR carries a
			displayId and so it must be redundant. That reasoning was wrong and it cost
			every collar in the game its place in the inventory. The collars were still
			created, still written to the database, and .tameprofile still listed the
			beast - but not one of them appeared in the player's bag, because without
			this the client has nothing to draw 9302 as.

			Stock item 118 ("Necklace of Magic") is a real item the client certainly
			knows how to render, so pointing at it is what gives a module item id an
			identity the client will accept. "No stock collar does this" is not the
			argument - no stock collar IS a module item, which is the entire situation
			this line exists to solve.
		-->
		<set name="displayId" val="118"/>
		<set name="etcitem_type" val="PET_COLLAR"/>
		<set name="default_action" val="SKILL_MAINTAIN"/>
		<set name="material" val="STEEL"/>
		<set name="weight" val="10"/>
		<set name="price" val="1"/>
		<set name="is_stackable" val="false"/>
		<set name="is_tradable" val="false"/>
		<set name="is_dropable" val="false"/>
		<set name="is_sellable" val="false"/>
		<set name="is_depositable" val="false"/>
		<set name="handler" val="DynamicPetSummon"/>
		<!--
			No <skills> block here, and that is deliberate.

			All eleven stock PET_COLLAR items bind skill 2046 "Wolves' Necklace", and
			this collar used to be the only one without any. That was diagnosed as the
			black square in the collar's skill GUI, and a <skills><skill id="2046"
			level="1"/></skills> was added to fix it. The theory was never actually
			confirmed on screen, and when the collar turned out to be invisible in the
			inventory, the safe move was to put this item back exactly as it was known
			to work rather than to keep an unverified edit on the same line.

			The black square is still an open problem. Whatever draws it, it is worth
			one screenshot and the exact beast, because "the client reserves a skill
			slot for the collar and has nothing to put in it" is a guess about a
			client this project does not own, and guessing here has already cost one
			working feature.

			For the record, and to stop this being "fixed" a third time on a hunch:
			2046 on this item would be display-only even if it were present, because
			stock collars cast it only through the SummonItems handler, which extends
			ItemSkillsTemplate and reads that list. DynamicPetSummon does not read it,
			it summons the collar's own individual from tamed_pet. So binding 2046
			cannot make a wrong beast appear - but neither has it been shown to remove
			anything.
		-->
	</item>

	<!--
		9303 - the reagent store, as an item the player double-clicks.

		WHY AN ITEM AT ALL
		The obvious choice was Adena: always in the bag, and double-clicking it does
		nothing at the moment. That does not work, and the reason is worth keeping.
		Item handlers are reached from the UseItem packet, and the Interlude client
		does not send UseItem for Adena. Currency is special-cased on the client
		side, so no packet means no server-side handler no matter what the server
		data says. It was tried properly - the module redefined item 57 with a
		handler - and nothing happened in game, so it was removed again rather than
		left in place redefining the game's currency for a feature that cannot fire.

		The collar is the proof that this approach does work: it is an ordinary
		EtcItem with a handler, and double-clicking it opens the passport.

		HOW THE LOOKUP WORKS, because it is by NAME and not by item id
		ItemHandler.registerHandler(h) stores a handler under
		h.getClass().getSimpleName(). ItemHandler.getHandler(item) looks it up with
		EtcItem.getHandlerName(), which is the <set name="handler"> value below.
		So the val here must stay exactly "TameStoreCatalogue", the simple name of
		the class in scripts/taming.

		UseItem does not consult default_action before calling the handler; it
		calls it for any EtcItem outside the paperdoll slot range. The sets below
		are therefore about looks and safety, not about making the click work.

		WHY displayId 1695
		1695 is stock "Spell Paper", icon etc_piece_of_paper_white_i00 - literally
		a sheet of paper, which is the look wanted for a catalogue.

		Know what this costs: AbstractItemPacket writes getDisplayId() and NO name
		string, so the client takes the icon AND the name from the display id. The
		player will see this item called "Spell Paper" in their bag, not "Taming
		Catalogue". The same is true of the other module items, which is why the
		collar borrows 118 and is drawn as a necklace. Changing the name means
		changing displayId to a stock item whose name reads better; there is no
		server-side name override for an item.
	-->
	<item id="9303" type="EtcItem" name="Taming Catalogue">
		<set name="icon" val="icon.etc_piece_of_paper_white_i00" />
		<set name="displayId" val="1695" />
		<set name="material" val="PAPER" />
		<!--
			No default_action and no etcitem_type. The collar sets both
			(default_action SKILL_MAINTAIN, etcitem_type PET_COLLAR) because it is a
			collar; nothing here needs them, and UseItem ignores default_action for
			handler dispatch anyway.
		-->
		<set name="price" val="0" />
		<set name="weight" val="0" />
		<set name="is_stackable" val="false" />
		<!--
			Not tradable, not droppable, not sellable, not depositable: this is a
			permanent permission slip, not loot. It is granted on login and should
			never leave the owner's bag or be handed to a merchant.
		-->
		<set name="is_tradable" val="false" />
		<set name="is_dropable" val="false" />
		<set name="is_sellable" val="false" />
		<set name="is_depositable" val="false" />
		<set name="handler" val="TameStoreCatalogue" />
	</item>

</list>
